{"name":"Cave of the Winds","hex":"#86736e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cave","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large, naturally-occurring cavity formed underground or in the face of a cliff or a hillside.","A hole, depression, or gap in earth or rock, whether natural or man-made."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surrender.","To collapse."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cave"},{"term":"winds","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of wind"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of wind"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/winds"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cave of the Winds (New York)","blocks":[{"text":"The Cave of the Winds was a natural cave behind Bridal Veil Falls at the Niagara Falls. The cave was some 130 feet (40 m) high, 100 feet (30 m) wide and 30 feet (9 m) in depth. It was discovered in 1834, and originally dubbed Aeolus's Cave, after the Greek god of winds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Entrance_to_Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_winter_%28NYPL_b11708197-G91F069_077F%29.jpg/330px-Entrance_to_Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_winter_%28NYPL_b11708197-G91F069_077F%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_the_Winds_(New_York)","wikidata":"Q2943304"},{"title":"Cave of the Winds (Colorado)","blocks":[{"text":"Cave of the Winds is a cave in the Pikes Peak region of Colorado. It is located just west of Colorado Springs on U.S. Highway 24, near the Manitou Cliff Dwellings. Tours of the complex of caves are given daily."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Cave_of_the_winds.jpg/330px-Cave_of_the_winds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_the_Winds_(Colorado)","wikidata":"Q5054965"},{"title":"Cave","blocks":[{"text":"Caves and caverns are natural voids under the surface of the Earth and have also been observed in other rocky worlds. Caves often form by the weathering of rock and can extend deep underground. Exogene caves are smaller openings that extend a relatively short distance underground. Caves which extend farther underground than the opening is wide are called endogene caves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Lechuguilla_Cave_Pearlsian_Gulf.jpg/330px-Lechuguilla_Cave_Pearlsian_Gulf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave","wikidata":"Q35509"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_by_Barker%2C_George%2C_1844-1894.jpg/120px-Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_by_Barker%2C_George%2C_1844-1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_by_Barker%2C_George%2C_1844-1894.jpg/250px-Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_by_Barker%2C_George%2C_1844-1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Cave_of_the_Winds%2C_by_Barker%2C_George%2C_1844-1894